13:38:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is a really common perception, but it doesn't hold up against the actual data. Let me break down why: **Media coverage is skewed** A widely-cited study (Kearns, Betus, and Lemieux, 2019) found that attacks by Muslim perpetrators in the US receive **357% more media coverage** than attacks by non-Muslims, even controlling for number of victims. So it's not that Muslim-perpetrated attacks happen more often — they just get dramatically more airtime, making them more memorable and giving a skewed sense of frequency. This is a classic case of the "availability heuristic" — we judge how common something is by how easily we can recall examples, and media coverage directly shapes that. **The actual numbers** - In the US, FBI and DHS data have repeatedly shown that **far-right/white nationalist extremism** has been the leading domestic terrorism threat for years — more attacks and more deaths than Islamist terrorism in most recent years. - Globally, the **overwhelming majority of victims of Islamist terrorism (like ISIS or Boko Haram) are themselves Muslims**. Groups like these have killed far more Muslims than people of any other faith. - Out of roughly **1.9 billion Muslims worldwide**, the number involved in terrorism is a minuscule fraction of a percent. **A useful comparison** When a white man commits a mass shooting, we don't usually hear "some Christian guy" or "some white guy" as the explanatory frame — his religion or race isn't treated as the reason. But when the perpetrator is Muslim, faith often becomes the headline explanation, reinforcing an association that isn't actually applied consistently. **Why the pattern feels real even though it isn't statistically** - Repetition in news coverage creates a mental shortcut, even if the underlying data doesn't support it. - A small number of dramatic, widely-covered incidents can outweigh a much larger number of less-covered ones in shaping perception. - Confirmation bias — once you have the association in mind, you may notice/remember cases that fit it more than cases that don't. So it's understandable why it *feels* like a pattern — but that feeling is more a product of media framing than an accurate reflection of who commits violence.
